title: "BORDER OPTICS: Satellite Verification of India's Vibrant Villages Programme"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
    description: "Zenodo archive of BORDER OPTICS v1.0.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
abstract: >-
  An independent satellite-verification study of India's Vibrant Villages
  Programme (VVP-I), a ₹4,800 crore border-area development scheme sanctioned
  across 2,967 villages in five Himalayan border states/union territories.
  Using Sentinel-2 built-up-index (NDBI) and VIIRS night-lights data at 258
  individually geocoded villages against a district-restricted non-VVP
  control group of 735 villages in the same 14 districts, this study tests
  whether VVP-I's priority villages show measurable physical development
  since programme sanction, under two independent compositing windows
  (full-year and summer-matched) as an explicit robustness check rather than
  trusting a single satellite comparison. An earlier version of this study
  found a significant summer-window built-up-area increase that survived
  every robustness check run against it; that result depended on an
  incomplete satellite extraction that had returned zero usable imagery for
  all 31 of Sikkim's villages, an archive-timing artifact rather than a
  permanent gap. Once a complete re-extraction was pulled the same day as the
  control group's own data, the result reversed to a clean null and stayed
  null against the control group, under leave-one-district-out and
  randomization-inference checks, and across a 250m/500m/1km buffer-radius
  sweep. Night-lights show no absolute before/after increase of their own in
  either window; against the control group, one fragile result survives — a
  full-year positive gap that holds under district fixed effects but not
  under an HC3 no-fixed-effects specification, rests on this study's worst
  treated-vs-control baseline imbalance, and holds in only 10 of 14
  leave-one-district-out reruns. A new, unstressed finding takes the old
  result's place: on the complete data, summer-window built-up change
  correlates with distance to the border/LAC (ρ = 0.291, p = 0.0000026) —
  opposite to the direction predicted by securitization theory, and flagged
  as open pending the same robustness checks already run on the retired
  result. The reversal itself, and the incomplete-extraction mechanism behind
  it, are reported as the central methodological finding, addressing a
  parliamentary record showing no independent impact assessment has ever been
  carried out for VVP-I.
keywords:
  - Vibrant Villages Programme
  - border development
  - securitization theory
  - satellite verification
  - NDBI
  - VIIRS night-lights
  - remote sensing
  - robustness testing
  - difference-in-differences
  - India
  - geospatial analysis
